## What the gates cost, and what they bought

**13 recorded attempts across four gates; 9 did not pass** (4 passing: plan.r3,
implement.r1, verify.r5, docs.r3). An earlier version of this artifact said
"eleven rounds, five did not pass" — a number I asserted without reading the
manifest, in a section about claims that were asserted without being checked.
The final review gate caught it.

- **plan r1, r2** (`rerun`) — self-granted exemptions to the acceptance, a test file read
  as one suite when it is three, an extraction dependency list missing the bd
  shim, and a verification grep anchored on `.sh` that structurally could not
  see a live wrong pointer.
- **verify** (`escalated` ×4, `rerun` ×2) — the artifact claimed the edited hook had been exercised by
  pushes (false: `core.hooksPath` resolves to the main checkout, whose copy is
  unedited), then, after correction, still opened by asserting the thing it
  retracted three paragraphs later.
- **docs** (`escalated`, `rerun`) — a replacement pointer attributing an error to `etude gate`
  that only `capture-gate` can emit, then a hook comment claiming
  `etude capture` / `etude gate` push refs when neither pushes anything.

Six of the nine are `escalated`, and every one of those is the same cause: the
L2/L4 opus seat has no working subprocess path, so `etude gate` fail-closed on an
incomplete panel. That is the machine behaving correctly, not a finding.

**Every substantive non-passing round was a false or incomplete CLAIM, not
defective code.**
That is worth stating plainly: a bead whose entire purpose is removing
documentation that describes a mechanism the repo no longer has spent five
rounds writing documentation that described mechanisms the repo does not have.
The sweep was disciplined about finding stale pointers and careless about
whether each replacement was true — which is the same failure one level down.
Docs r2 is the sharpest instance: the original comment was stale but TRUE, and
the rewrite made it false.

Two rounds also produced work beyond corrections:

- The `review-gate-runbook.md` section was stale on two counts nobody had
  flagged — it asserted docs is not a captured stage and prescribed the
  transport-only alias `etude-1od` had removed two beads earlier. A live
  judgement runbook was instructing readers to do the thing this epic had just
  eliminated.
- Docs r2 exposed an operational gap: the BLOCKED message told an operator to
  capture and gate, but the audit it fronts GAPs on `unpushed-ref`, so following
  the message exactly left them still blocked. The message now names the push
  step, and the supervisor runbook's close checklist names `etude sync` so the
  loop closes where the message points.

## The gap the final review found

Three real in-harness opus verdicts had been acted on without being recorded:
the verify-r1 BLOCK on artifact `8b1382a7`, the verify-r2 GO on `9d9a4d42`
(which made that round a genuine split — codex BLOCK, opus GO — rather than the
one-sided round the manifest showed), and the docs-r2 BLOCK on `d3928647`.

The last of those is the one that matters. It produced the sharpest finding of
this bead — that a stale-but-true pointer in the repo's push gate had been
replaced with a false one — and both this artifact and the docs artifact quote it
as the central lesson. Its verdict existed only in a transcript.

**A gate whose findings live only in a transcript is exactly the defect this
epic exists to end**, reproduced by the bead that removes it. So all three were
recorded through the bootstrap path (`verify.r6`, `verify.r7`, `docs.r4`), each
naming in `reviewed_stages` the artifact revision actually reviewed, since the
round number is only an attempt counter. Every stage artifact on this run now
carries a gate attempt except the pre-revision plan draft, which was corrected
before it was ever gated, and this review artifact, which is being gated now.

Worth stating: `scripts/dogfood-completeness-audit.sh` does not detect this class
of gap at all, and bead `.4` shrinks that audit further. Nothing mechanical would
have caught it — a seat did.

## Honest limits

- **The edited hook is not the executing hook from here.** `core.hooksPath`
  points at the main checkout's `.beads/hooks`; this worktree's copy becomes
  live only once this lands and that copy is updated. Nothing detects drift
  between them — filed as `etude-6d9`.
- **Nothing runs the shell suites automatically.** No CI exists, and `make test`
  is `go test ./...`. `shell-test` gives them one command, not enforcement.
- **`make dogfood-audit` does not pass.** Two causes: pre-existing
  `cadence-sidecar` debt that bead `.4` removes, and an `unpushed-ref` gap on
  this bead's own run ref whenever a stage or gate has been captured since the
  last push — which clears on `etude sync`, and which the pre-push hook will
  enforce before the code push lands. (The audit labels that gap "diverged"; it
  is fast-forwardable.) No gap count is quoted: the audit scans a moving
  `--last 9` window, and two earlier versions of this claim went stale before a
  seat could check them.

